---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import Src from "../components/Src.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import register from "../data/register.json";
const rows = register.filter((r) => (r.surname || "").toUpperCase() === "RIVETTI");
const swept = rows.filter((r) => r.kind === "swept").length;
---
<Base title="Rivetti"
      eyebrow="Generation two married a Rivetti"
      dek="Chiara Rivetti, who outlived Pasquale Falco by nine years — and the two Rivetti families of Arienzo, one of them gentry, that the archive spent five years telling apart.">

  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">
    <p><strong>Chiara Rivetti was the oldest open question this archive had</strong>, and closing it in
    2026 was the single biggest find of the register sweeps. She still had no page. This is it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Chiara Rivetti</h2>
  <p class="lede">She married <P n="Pasquale Falco" bold={true} /> in the 1780s and was still alive on
  <strong>strada Camellara</strong> when he died there on 1 October 1834. She outlived him by nearly
  nine years, and died in her own house at eight in the evening on <strong>3 September 1843</strong>,
  aged eighty.</p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>…è morta nella sua propria casa <strong>CHIARA RIVETTI VEDOVA DI PASQUALE FALCO</strong>, nata
    in Arienzo, di anni <strong>OTTANTA</strong>, di professione <strong>CONTADINA</strong>, domiciliata
    ivi, <strong>figlia di NICOLA RIVETTI Defunto, di professione CONTADINO</strong>, domiciliato ivi,
    <strong>e di ANNA DI RUGGIO Defunta</strong>, domiciliata ivi.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p class="small">Arienzo, Morti 1843, atto 43. Two Cioffi of strada Camellara walked to the town hall
  to report it. Plate on the <a href={u("/documents")}>Documents</a> page.</p>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.1rem 0">
    <p><strong>And a second series now says the same thing, twenty-nine years earlier.</strong> Her
    son Vincenzo's marriage dossier of 1814 — found in September 2026 — contains a notarial act of
    <strong>8 December 1814</strong> in which «li conjugi Pasquale Falco del fu Matteo, e
    <strong>CHIARA RIVETTI del fu NICOLA</strong>» consent to their son's marriage. A notary's
    instrument and a parish burial, three decades and two record series apart, agree on her father.
    <Src plate="falco-consent-1814-act85.jpg" label="the act" /></p>
    <p class="small">The same dossier gives her an age for the first time outside a death act. The
    banns of 11 September 1814 make her <strong>forty-seven</strong>, implying a birth about
    <strong>1767</strong>. Her 1843 burial implied <strong>1763</strong>; the family tree says
    <strong>1772</strong>. <strong>Three estimates, no two agreeing</strong> — and in the same act the
    clerk gets her son's age and her son's bride's age right to the year. Her baptism is the next
    target. <a href={u("/processetti")}>The Processetti →</a></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why one word in that act mattered so much</h2>
  <p>Her Latin burial entry called her only <em>«filia quondam NICOLAI et ANNA»</em> — daughter of the
  late Nicola and Anna. <strong>Two Arienzo couples answered to that.</strong></p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Couple</th><th>Who they were</th><th>What the archive did</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Don NICOLA RIVETTI</strong> × <P n="Donn'Anna Quotolo" bold={true} /></td>
        <td><strong>Gentry.</strong> She died on 25 April 1824, aged eighty-eight, a <em>gentildonna</em>
        of strada Croce dell'Ospedale, daughter of the late <strong>Silvestro Quotolo,
        possidente</strong>. Their daughter <P n="Donna Francesca Rivetti" bold={true} /> died in January
        1816, wife of <strong>Don Claudio Cimmino</strong>.</td>
        <td>Ruled out.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>NICOLA RIVETTI, <em>contadino</em></strong> × <P n="Anna di Ruggio" bold={true} /></td>
        <td><strong>Day-labourers.</strong> Named in the 1821 death act of their son <P n="Carmine Rivetti" bold={true} />, buried at fifty-three from <strong>strada Monticello de' Cappuccini</strong>.</td>
        <td><strong>Chiara's parents.</strong> The 1843 act writes her father's trade out —
        <em>contadino</em> — and that single word decides it.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The archive had preferred the labouring couple for years, on the reasoning that Chiara married a
  <em>contadino</em> and lived among day-labourers — <strong>and refused to claim it, because a
  preference is not a record</strong>. It waited for the word. <strong>And it makes CARMINE RIVETTI,
  buried in 1821 at fifty-three, her brother</strong>, born about 1768.</p>

  <h2>The other Rivetti of Arienzo — the ones with the Don</h2>
  <p>The gentry Rivetti are worth knowing precisely so that nobody mistakes them for Chiara's people.
  They are a different family in the same town, and the registers keep them apart with an honorific.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>What the register says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Don GIOVANBATTISTA RIVETTI</strong></td>
        <td><em>«FARMACISTA»</em> — <strong>the apothecary</strong>. Married Donna Caterina Espositella,
        a <em>gentildonna</em> of strada Porta di Sopra. Their three-year-old son Raffaele Ottaviano is
        entered as a <em>gentiluomo</em>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Don Giuseppe Rivetti" bold={true} /> the elder × Giovanna Bega</td>
        <td>Both dead before March 1819.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Don Nicola Rivetti" bold={true} /> × Donn'Anna Quotolo</td>
        <td>See above. She was eighty-eight at her death in 1824.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A Rivetti in a marriage record</td>
        <td>Styled <em><strong>Doctoris Physici</strong></em> — a physician.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small"><strong>An apothecary, a physician and three Don in one surname</strong>, alongside
  a household of <em>contadini</em> who buried their dead from Monticello de' Cappuccini. Same name,
  same parish, two worlds.</p>

  <h2>A Rivetti married a Falco daughter too</h2>
  <p><P n="Crescenzo Rivetti" bold={true} /> married <P n="Angela Falco" bold={true} /> — a daughter of
  <strong>Matteo Falco and Francesca Crisci</strong>, so a sister of Pasquale of generation two.
  Their daughter Angelandrea Rivetti died on 12 February 1819. When Crescenzo died in December 1816,
  <strong>Gioacchino Falco (36) and Giuseppe Falco (32), both of strada Camellara, declared it as
  «parenti del defunto»</strong> — they were his wife's brothers, and that stated word is what makes
  Gioacchino a candidate fifth child of the founding couple.</p>
  <p><strong>So the two names cross in both directions in the founding generation</strong>: Chiara
  Rivetti marries in, Angela Falco marries out.</p>

  <h2>Two Giuseppe Rivetti of standing — and it is not settled</h2>
  <p class="lede">This archive has carried a muddle here for some time. It can now be stated exactly,
  which is not the same as solved.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>The man</th><th>His wife</th><th>Known from</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Don GIUSEPPE RIVETTI the elder</strong><br/><span class="small">dead before March 1819</span></td>
        <td><strong>GIOVANNA BEGA</strong><br/><span class="small">also dead by then</span></td>
        <td class="small">The 1819 death of their daughter <P n="Suor Maria Maddalena Rivetti" bold={true} />, a nun.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>GIUSEPPE RIVETTI, <em>possidente</em></strong><br/><span class="small">dead before November 1839</span></td>
        <td><strong>LUCIA CIOFFI</strong><br/><span class="small">also dead by then</span></td>
        <td class="small">The 1839 death of their son <strong>Don GIOVANBATTISTA RIVETTI, <em>FARMACISTA</em></strong>, aged 55 — so born about 1784.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="card" style="margin:1.1rem 0">
    <p><strong>First, the name is now certain.</strong> The 1839 act was read at 3400 pixels and gave
    «LUCIA COPPA(?)/CIOFFI» — a question mark and an alternative. Re-fetched at the image's own
    resolution it reads «<strong>e di Lucia Cioffi Defunta</strong>», and the double-f is unmistakable.
    <strong>Lucia Coppa never existed</strong>; she was a misreading.
    <a href={u("/corrections")}>Corrections →</a></p>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>What is not certain is whether these are two men or one.</strong>
    Two Giuseppe Rivetti of standing, both dead, with two different wives. Either there were two, or
    one man married twice. <strong>Nothing found so far decides it</strong>, and this archive will not
    pick the tidier answer. What would settle it: the marriage records, or a death act for either
    Giuseppe that names his own parents.</p>
  </div>
  <p><strong>And it puts the Rivetti and the Cioffi together twice, in both directions.</strong>
  Giuseppe Rivetti the <em>possidente</em> married a <strong>Lucia CIOFFI</strong>; and in February
  1815 a <strong>Don Saverio CIOFFI</strong> was married to a <strong>Donna Anna Maria RIVETTI</strong>,
  whose seven-day-old daughter Metilde died that month and is entered as a <em>gentildonna</em>. The
  gentry of this town married each other.</p>
  <p class="small"><strong>Neither is this line.</strong> Ours are <em>contadini</em>, and
  <P n="Chiara Rivetti" bold={true} /> was the daughter of a contadino — the question this archive
  spent five years on, and answered no.</p>

  <h2>The Rivetti on the ground, 1816–1843</h2>
  <p>{swept} of these {rows.length} records were read off the death registers line by line. What they
  show is a family spread across Arienzo — <strong>Monticello de' Cappuccini</strong>, Via Nuova,
  Capodiuomo, Sant'Andrea, San Giuseppe, and <strong>strada Camellara</strong> itself.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>What the act says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><P n="Alessio Rivetti" bold={true} /> × <P n="Orsola Diglio" bold={true} />, strada Camellara</td>
        <td><strong>Wife and daughter dead on the same day.</strong> Grazia Rivetti, nine years old, at
        six in the morning on 28 August 1842; her mother Orsola, thirty-six, four hours earlier. The
        same two men reported both.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A Rivetti of strada Camellara × <P n="Chiara Sorrino" bold={true} /></td>
        <td><strong>Two children dead in two days</strong> — Paolo, three, on 23 August; Grazia, seven
        months, on the 24th.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Felicia Rivetti" bold={true} /> × <P n="Vincenzo Morgillo" bold={true} /></td>
        <td>Died 30 January 1839 at thirty-seven, <em>«figlia di ANTONIO RIVETTI»</em>, a
        <em>contadino</em> still living, <em>«e di Marchetta Quotolo»</em>. <strong>A Rivetti woman
        married into a Morgillo household</strong> — the other name that flanks this line. See
        <a href={u("/morgillo")}>Morgillo</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Angelo Rivetti" bold={true} />, 28, <em>calzolajo</em></td>
        <td>Declares at the death of Salvatore Rivetti, 58, <em>vaticale</em> of strada Sant'Andrea —
        and the register calls him <em><strong>NIPOTE</strong></em>, nephew. <strong>A stated kinship,
        not an inferred one.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Antonio Rivetti" bold={true} />, 40, of strada Via Nuova</td>
        <td>Reports the death of his own twenty-day-old son Paolo, and the clerk writes
        <em><strong>PADRE</strong></em>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>What would move this line</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The marriage of Pasquale Falco and Chiara Rivetti</strong>, Arienzo, 1780s. It would
    name all four parents at once and is the oldest document this archive is still looking for.</li>
    <li><strong>Nicola Rivetti and Anna di Ruggio's own marriage</strong>, about 1760, which would take
    the labouring Rivetti back another generation.</li>
    <li><strong>Whether the two Rivetti families are one family that divided.</strong> An apothecary
    and a day-labourer of one surname in one small town is not two families by default — but nothing
    found so far joins them, and the archive does not assume it.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">All {rows.length} Rivetti records are listed with their
  sources at <a href={u("/register") + "?q=Rivetti"}>the register →</a></p>
</Base>
